.SH A) OVERVIEW
executables (exec-steps) are the calling of functions for testing and reviewing
the results.  these are the bread-and-butter of unit-testing.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
exec-steps are translated function calls, result evaluation, and statistic
capture within a condition and script.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
exec-steps have six fields.  the leading spaces are changed to spacers (·)
in this manual for clarity.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § run  § yLOG_enter  § "here"      § z_equal  § 0              §
   ·····get   § chk  § ylog__unit  § "current"   § s_equal  § yLOG_enter     §
   ·····echo  § ret  § - - - - - - § [[ s_str ]] § w_equal  § åyLOG_enteræ   §

EXEC and GET work identically, they are separated to help statistics of actual
function calls vs testing results.

.SH D) VERB
the verbs EXEC, GET, and ECHO and are is expected to be indented five (5)
characters off-estting it from SCRP and later steps.  the verb field are
is twelve (12) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····get   § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····echo  § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

since descriptions are demonstrated elsewhere, i have truncated them greatly
to fit everything in the available space ;)

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § run  § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····get   § chk  § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····echo  § ret  § ·           § ·           § ·        § ·              §

.SH F) FUNCTION
the third field is the actual function name to be called, except in ECHO it
is blank.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § yLOG_enter  § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····get   § ·    § ylog__unit  § ·           § ·        § ·              §
   ·····echo  § ·    § - - - - - - § ·           § ·        § ·              §

.SH G) PARAMS
the fourth field is the parameters to be passed to the function, formatted
exactly as in a real call.  separate parameters with commas (,) exactly like
real calls.  ECHO is a variable name (included in double square brackest)
to be interrogated.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § ·           § "here"      § ·        § ·              §
   ·····get   § ·    § ·           § "current"   § ·        § ·              §
   ·····echo  § ·    § ·           § [[ s_str ]] § ·        § ·              §

.SH H) TEST
the fifth field is the method to test the result against the expect value.
these are all provided by the yVAR library and are explained there.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § ·           § ·           § z_equal  § ·              §
   ·····get   § ·    § ·           § ·           § s_equal  § ·              §
   ·····echo  § ·    § ·           § ·           § w_equal  § ·              §

refer to yVAR for all the possible tests, but the most common are...
   i_equal        integer equality ==
   i_lesser       integer lesser than <
   i_greater      integer greater than >
   r_equal        real/float equality ==
   r_lesser       real/float lesser than <
   r_greater      real/float greater than >
   s_equal        string equality, plus many cool extras
   w_equal        string equality, plus many cool extras with å...æ wrappers
   s_not          string inequality (strcmp != 0)
   p_null         pointer is grounded/NULL
   p_exists       pointer is not grounded/NULL
   u_round        rounding numbers inside a string (greybox)

.SH I) EXPECT
the sixth field is the expected results, no quotes are required for strings
as the field will be interpreted based on the TEST requested.

   verb------ § desc § --function- § ---parms--- § --test-- § ---expect----- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § 0              §
   ·····get   § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § yLOG_enter     §
   ·····echo  § ·    § ·           § ·           § ·        § åyLOG_enteræ   §

.SH J) RETURN (uncommon)
an optional seventh field is a variable to capture the return value for use in
later steps.  this is useful (especially pointers) to make sure a value changed
from the previous setting even though the new value can not be predicted.

   verb------ § desc § -function- § -parms- § -test- § -expect- § --return-- §
   ·····exec  § ·    § ·          § ·       § ·      § ·        § x_string   §

.SH K) OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, each step generates a block of output.  the output
says it is the 3rd step in the condition (ac) and is generated by line 211 in
the source code.  everything else is a reflection of input values.  this line
will also be "green" and say PASS is it passed or be "red" and say FAIL if
it failed for fast visual scanning.

   ···ac) PASS   : call presort ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·[00211]
   ·······method : yLOG_enter ("here")
   ·······test   : z_equal    (rc =  101, test abbr = e)
   ·······expect :   1å0æ
   ·······actual :   1å0æ

NOTE: this output can be much shorter or a little longer depending on how
koios was called.

NOTE: if the line number is [00555] is is in a specific unit test source file.
if like å00555æ it is in a shared file -- unit_data.unit or unit_wide.unit.
